 4. Anthropic to investors: Trump hates us so much 
 
 By Maria Curi 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Anthropic is warning investors that it may continue to be a target of the Trump administration as it seeks one of the biggest IPOs in history — and that it has no plans to change its behavior. 

 Why it matters: The filing doesn't name the president or his administration specifically, but the subtext is clear. Anthropic outlines the risk of being "disproportionately or selectively targeted" due to its positions on AI safety.

 Between the lines: Anthropic's clashes with the Trump administration have been as much about personalities as policy.

 The company has refused to yield on AI safety and also worked to patch up administration squabbles , including by dispatching co-founder Tom Brown to work with Trump officials. Amodei also attended a White House event last month.

 In the filing, Anthropic didn't commit to changing the advocacy and policy positions that have landed it in the Trump administration's crosshairs.

 Anthropic says it has engaged in political advocacy, supported political organizations and publicly weighed in on AI policy and regulation, activities it expects to continue. Keep reading .

 
 
